course: dictionary
lesson: 11924
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and calculus is one of them. You can find the right meaning by reading the words around it, which are its context. «Maya is learning calculus, the maths that helps engineers work out how fast a rocket speeds up.» The clues are maths and rocket, so this calculus is a branch of maths.
explain: «The dentist scraped hard calculus off my teeth.» Now the clues are dentist and teeth. Calculus must mean the hard, sticky stuff that builds up on teeth, not a kind of maths. Always ask: does my meaning fit the sentence?
explain: Maths calculus is usually not counted, so we do not say calculuses. When calculus means a hard stone in the body, the plural is calculi.
fact: Calculus is a branch of advanced maths.
fact: Calculus studies how things change, and how to add up endless tiny pieces.
fact: «Calculus» can also mean a hard stone that forms in the body, such as a kidney stone.
fact: At the dentist, «calculus» means hard tartar, the stuff that builds up on teeth.
fact: Isaac Newton and Gottfried Leibniz invented calculus in the 1600s.
exercise: «Maya is learning calculus, the maths of rockets and speed.» What is calculus in this sentence? | A kind of sweet food | A type of tooth | *A branch of advanced maths | A hard stone in the body
exercise: «The dentist scraped calculus off my teeth.» What is calculus in this sentence? | A very difficult maths test | *Hard tartar that builds up on teeth | A minty toothpaste | A sticky sweet sauce
exercise: «Doctors found a small ____ in his kidney, and it was very painful.» Which word fits the gap? | algebra | biology | geometry | *calculus
test: «Newton and Leibniz invented calculus in the 1600s.» Who invented calculus? | *Isaac Newton and Gottfried Leibniz | Two famous dentists from London | Two kings who ruled in the 1900s | Two astronauts who flew to the Moon
test: «Calculus helps scientists understand how a speed or a curve changes.» What does calculus study? | How plants grow in the dark | *How things change | How to spell long words | How to cook rice
exam: The plural of calculus, when it means a hard stone in the body, is ____. | calculis | calculae | calculuses | *calculi
